# Example environment file.
#
# Copy to .env:
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# DEVELOPMENT
# -----------
# When OAUTH_ENV is dev/development/local/test, the server generates ephemeral
# values for OAUTH_KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEY and OAUTH_AUDIT_PEPPER if they are unset, so
# a fresh checkout boots. Generated secrets are per-process: encrypted rows (TOTP
# secrets, client secrets) do NOT survive a restart. That is fine for development
# and unacceptable anywhere else.
#
# PRODUCTION
# ----------
# Both secrets MUST be set. Each is 32 bytes, given as 64 hex characters. Generate:
#     openssl rand -hex 32
# The loader refuses to start if either is missing, still an example value, or the
# wrong length.
#
# ENCRYPTION KEY  -> OAUTH_KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEY   (must decode to exactly 32 bytes)
# AUDIT PEPPER    -> OAUTH_AUDIT_PEPPER         (must decode to at least 32 bytes)
#
# The application reads configuration from config.yaml; the values below are
# consumed by docker-compose and by the process environment. Every application
# setting can be overridden with an OAUTH_-prefixed variable, for example
# OAUTH_SERVER_ISSUER or OAUTH_DATABASE_URL.

# Database. In docker-compose the host is the service name `postgres`.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://oauth:oauth@localhost:5432/oauth?sslmode=disable

# Redis. In docker-compose the host is the service name `redis`.
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0

# Issuer. Must match the browser-facing base URL. https outside development.
SERVER_ISSUER=http://localhost:8080

# Secrets. Leave unset in development to use generated values; set both in any
# real deployment. Example (DO NOT USE THESE) — replace with `openssl rand -hex 32`:
# OAUTH_KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# OAUTH_AUDIT_PEPPER=

# Email. docker-compose runs MailHog, whose SMTP port is 1025.
SMTP_HOST=localhost
SMTP_PORT=1025
